# 12. SMART CUTAWAY IMPLEMENTATION LOGIC

Inspect the existing scene hierarchy first.

Use stable semantic wall groups.

For example, identify:

- Exterior walls.
- Back walls.
- Near-camera walls.
- Interior shared walls.
- Door frames.
- Permanent window frames.
- Structural pillars.
- Optional decorative wall panels.

Do not rely only on mesh name substrings if existing naming is inconsistent.

Use actual metadata or explicit wall-role registration where possible.

## OCCLUSION DETECTION

Use camera position and agent/interior target positions.

Potential technique:

- Cast a ray from the camera toward the visible bounds of an agent.
- Identify wall meshes blocking the view.
- Determine whether the wall is eligible for temporary cutaway.
- Fade or hide only eligible wall sections.
- Leave non-occluding structural elements intact.

For each actor, consider the head and torso, not only a single foot-level point.

Where appropriate, also protect the view of essential furniture and workstation activity.

## PERFORMANCE

Do not perform expensive full-scene raycasting unnecessarily every animation frame.

Update cutaway decisions when:

- Camera changes meaningfully.
- Full Office mode starts.
- Room selection changes.
- Character moves enough to change occlusion.
- Meeting mode changes.
- The wall visibility mode changes.

Use throttling or efficient scene queries.

## MATERIAL SAFETY

If a wall is temporarily faded:

- Avoid changing a material shared by other walls unintentionally.
- Avoid transparency sorting artifacts.
- Avoid flickering during visibility transitions.
- Preserve original material properties.
- Restore all original states correctly.

A direct visibility toggle may be preferable to transparency for some structural elements.

Choose based on actual rendered results.

Do not hide doorframes, floors or essential structural pillars indiscriminately.

